    "core_content": {
      "parsing_res_list": [
        {
          "block_label": "image",
          "block_content": "+V \nR2\nWW Q2\nR1\n\nQ1\nGPIO pin LOAD \nGND \n\n",
          "block_bbox": [
            297,
            221,
            1799,
            627
          ],
          "block_id": 0,
          "block_order": null
        },
        {
          "block_label": "figure_title",
          "block_content": "Figure 11-4. High-side Switching with an NPN BJT Driving a PNP BJT ",
          "block_bbox": [
            292,
            644,
            1505,
            690
          ],
          "block_id": 1,
          "block_order": null
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "Low-side switching (Recipe 11.1) is the most common and simplest arrangement,and unless you have a good reason such as the need for one end of the load to be connected to ground, you should use low-side switching.\n",
          "block_bbox": [
            289,
            749,
            1798,
            898
          ],
          "block_id": 2,
          "block_order": 1
        },
        {
          "block_label": "paragraph_title",
          "block_content": "See Also ",
          "block_bbox": [
            291,
            951,
            477,
            1000
          ],
          "block_id": 3,
          "block_order": 2
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "For a discussion of NPN and PNP bipolar transistors, see Recipe 5.1.",
          "block_bbox": [
            289,
            1042,
            1496,
            1085
          ],
          "block_id": 4,
          "block_order": 3
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "GPIO ports and their output logic are described in Recipe 10.7.",
          "block_bbox": [
            290,
            1119,
            1405,
            1163
          ],
          "block_id": 5,
          "block_order": 4
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "You will find an example of switching with a transistor using an Arduino in Recipe 11.6 and for a Raspberry Pi in Recipe 11.7.\n",
          "block_bbox": [
            290,
            1197,
            1798,
            1291
          ],
          "block_id": 6,
          "block_order": 5
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "For switching using a MOSFET, see Recipe 11.3.",
          "block_bbox": [
            292,
            1327,
            1140,
            1372
          ],
          "block_id": 7,
          "block_order": 6
        },
        {
          "block_label": "paragraph_title",
          "block_content": "11.3 Switch Much More Power ",
          "block_bbox": [
            294,
            1429,
            1077,
            1490
          ],
          "block_id": 8,
          "block_order": 7
        },
        {
          "block_label": "paragraph_title",
          "block_content": "Problem ",
          "block_bbox": [
            291,
            1553,
            482,
            1603
          ],
          "block_id": 9,
          "block_order": 8
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "You want to allow a GPIO pin to control more power than it otherwise could, but a BJT isn't enough.\n",
          "block_bbox": [
            290,
            1643,
            1798,
            1739
          ],
          "block_id": 10,
          "block_order": 9
        },
        {
          "block_label": "paragraph_title",
          "block_content": "Solution ",
          "block_bbox": [
            291,
            1796,
            479,
            1844
          ],
          "block_id": 11,
          "block_order": 10
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "You can use a MOSFET as an electronic switch. Use the transistor in a commonsource arrangement. Figure 11-5 shows the schematicfor this circuit. Along with Recipe11.1, you will find yourself using this circuit a lot.",
          "block_bbox": [
            288,
            1886,
            1798,
            2035
          ],
          "block_id": 12,
          "block_order": 11
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "This type of switching is called \"low-side\"switching because the transistor acts as a switch between the low voltage of GND and the load.\n",
          "block_bbox": [
            288,
            2068,
            1798,
            2162
          ],
          "block_id": 13,
          "block_order": 12
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "If the GPIO pin is high (3.3V or 5V) and exceeds the gate-threshold voltage of the MOSFET, the MOSFET will turn on, allowing current to flow from +V through the load to GND.\n",
          "block_bbox": [
            289,
            2198,
            1798,
            2342
          ],
          "block_id": 14,
          "block_order": 13
        },
        {
          "block_label": "footer",
          "block_content": "11.3 Switch Much More Power ",
          "block_bbox": [
            1298,
            2546,
            1669,
            2577
          ],
          "block_id": 15,
          "block_order": null
        },
        {
          "block_label": "number",
          "block_content": "173",
          "block_bbox": [
            1752,
            2547,
            1796,
            2577
          ],
          "block_id": 16,
          "block_order": null
        }
      ],
      "markdown": "<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"imgs/img_in_image_box_297_221_1799_627.jpg\" alt=\"Image\" width=\"71%\" /></div>\n\n\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\">Figure 11-4. High-side Switching with an NPN BJT Driving a PNP BJT </div>\n\n\nLow-side switching (Recipe 11.1) is the most common and simplest arrangement,and unless you have a good reason such as the need for one end of the load to be connected to ground, you should use low-side switching.\n\n\n\n## See Also \n\nFor a discussion of NPN and PNP bipolar transistors, see Recipe 5.1.\n\nGPIO ports and their output logic are described in Recipe 10.7.\n\nYou will find an example of switching with a transistor using an Arduino in Recipe 11.6 and for a Raspberry Pi in Recipe 11.7.\n\n\n\nFor switching using a MOSFET, see Recipe 11.3.\n\n### 11.3 Switch Much More Power \n\n## Problem \n\nYou want to allow a GPIO pin to control more power than it otherwise could, but a BJT isn't enough.\n\n\n\n## Solution \n\nYou can use a MOSFET as an electronic switch. Use the transistor in a commonsource arrangement. Figure 11-5 shows the schematicfor this circuit. Along with Recipe11.1, you will find yourself using this circuit a lot.\n\nThis type of switching is called \"low-side\"switching because the transistor acts as a switch between the low voltage of GND and the load.\n\n\n\nIf the GPIO pin is high (3.3V or 5V) and exceeds the gate-threshold voltage of the MOSFET, the MOSFET will turn on, allowing current to flow from +V through the load to GND.\n\n",
      "tables": [],
      "formulas": [],
      "seals": []
    },
